 "brief": "Complexity, action, and black holes — Our earlier paper “Complexity Equals Action” conjectured that the quantum computational complexity of a holographic state is given by the classical action of a",
 "extended": "**Complexity, action, and black holes**\n\n*Adam R. Browna, Daniel A. Robertsb, Leonard Susskinda*\n\n70 pp, 47 sections.\n\n**Abstract.** Our earlier paper “Complexity Equals Action” conjectured that the quantum computational complexity of a holographic state is given by the classical action of a region in the bulk (the “Wheeler-DeWitt” patch). We provide calculations for the results quoted in that paper, explain how it ﬁts into a broader (tensor) network of ideas, and elaborate on the hypothesis that black holes are the fastest computers in nature. arXiv:1512.04993v2 [hep-th] 10 May 2016\n\n**Outline.** Front matter; 1 Introduction; 1.1 Complexity equals action; 2 Bounds on information storage and processing; 2.2 Bounds on information scrambling or chaos; 2.3 Bounds on quantum evolution; 2.4 Complexity; 2.5 Conjectured complexity growth bound; 2.6 Bound on complexity growth with a conserve; 3 Action growth of black holes; 3.1 Action of an uncharged black hole; 3.2 Action of a charged black hole; 3.3 Action of a rotating BTZ black hole; 4 Testing our conjectures with black holes; 4.1 Neutral static black holes; 4.2 Rotating BTZ black holes; 4.3 Small charged black holes; 4.4 Intermediate and large charged black holes",
